          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state and intensifies that avowal through comparisons claiming unsurpassable love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression asserts present love but does not undertake a future action, obligation, or relational commitment. Lear rewards the answer with territory, but that reward does not convert Goneril’s avowal into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The love declaration is part of an extended, rhetorically structured answer to Lear’s request, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged verbal response. Its hyperbole does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Dearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions as competitive courtly praise and as an answer intended to qualify Goneril for Lear’s bounty. The supplied text supports this strategic situational function without establishing that the avowal is knowingly false.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters which loves him most and explicitly connects their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Goneril, called upon first, answers with an elaborate declaration that she loves Lear beyond other valued goods and relationships.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril undertakes no identifiable future action or obligation in the focal expression. Lear accepts the speech as a satisfactory answer and immediately grants her and Albany’s line a portion of the kingdom, but this is uptake of her declaration within the love test, not uptake of a promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The assessment uses only the supplied text and does not rely on later plot events.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage establishes the avowal’s public and competitive setting but does not settle Goneril’s sincerity or actual affection. Cordelia’s contrasting asides and objections invite doubt about the sisters’ rhetoric, but they do not conclusively prove Goneril’s inner state.\"}"
        }
